task: flink-stream-bench
agent: opus-flink
started: 2026-09-10
branch: feature/flink-stream-bench
worktree: ../okay-wt-flink-stream-bench
spec: docs/benchmarks.md (the new section), specs/external-systems.md P4

why: the operator asked for a comparison of our streams against Apache
  Flink on a workload that is actually hard, and chose the Wrocław
  timetable as its source. okay-flink so far carries ONE function and
  no number: `toFlink` claims an okay `Aggregator` IS a Flink
  `AggregateFunction`, and nothing has ever run it under a real
  engine. The claim makes the benchmark unusually fair — the
  aggregation VALUE is the same object in both lanes, so what the two
  numbers differ by is the engine, not the arithmetic.

what: a deterministic event-time stream derived from Wrocław's GTFS
  (vehicle, route, stop, scheduled minute, delay), replayed with
  out-of-order jitter, and one pipeline computed twice:
  (1) 5-minute tumbling windows per route — count+mean+max delay by
      ONE `Aggregator.zip`, handed to Flink through `toFlink`;
  (2) 15/1-minute sliding windows per stop, with a bunching detector
      (two vehicles of a route inside 2 minutes) — keyed state;
  (3) enrichment against the routes table;
  (4) top-K routes by mean delay per window.
  Both lanes assert the SAME answer (a checksum over window results)
  before either is timed. Flink lanes: local MiniCluster, parallelism
  1 and 4, checkpointing off and on (the guarantee priced, not
  assumed away); okay lanes: one JVM, Chunks/Pipe, and the merged
  multi-fibre shape.

predict: okay wins per element by a wide margin in one JVM (no
  serialization, no network, no checkpoint barriers) and the
  interesting number is not that — it is what the guarantee costs and
  where Flink's window machinery stops being overhead (bigger key
  space, spilling state). Report both, and state plainly what Flink
  buys that an in-process stream does not provide.
